92186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086214 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086214 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086214 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e7af1850-4481-5ae1-b808-903637f29a31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087491 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087491 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\ln(2)}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "effc520d-4717-5e46-9358-c2ea34c7aa24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087491 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} & ≈\\frac{4}{0.693\\,15} \\\\  & ≈5.770\\,76 \\\\  & ≈5.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087492 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087492 #edb/ref 1071 "Convert the expression $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ to its equivalent exponential form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97f9f0d6-d948-5f9b-b0ae-57e6a456a53b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087492 #edb/ref 1074 "The natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\ln{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = e^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ with $\\ln{a} = x,$ we get $a = 3$ and $x = 1.1.$\n\nTherefore, the approximation $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\ne^{1.1} \\approx 3 .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087493 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087493 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\ln\\left( e^{6}\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd02e00f-9e2c-54f1-a5d3-fb0da9c9a413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087493 #edb/ref 1074 "The natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = 6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{6}\\right) = 6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087497 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087497 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^{3}}\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37ca3b87-9f5f-583c-95ff-83ca35608559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087497 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{e^3}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( \\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left( e^{-3} \\right).\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( e^{-3}\\right) = - 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating Expressions Containing Natural Logarithms Using a Calculator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087683 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8d82c39c-5ec6-5cf3-96f8-e778091a451a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Natural Logarithms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087684 #edb/ref 